  <!-- CONTENT SECTION (SEO 800+ Words) -->
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none dark:prose-invert k-content">
      <h2>
        Complete Guide to 401(k) Retirement Plans: Compounding, Match, and
        Strategies
      </h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>401(k) plan</strong> is an employer-sponsored, tax-advantaged defined-contribution
        retirement account named after subsection 401(k) of the United States Internal
        Revenue Code. For decades, it has served as the cornerstone of American retirement
        planning, enabling millions of working professionals to automatically deduct
        a percentage of their pre-tax or post-tax salary and invest it directly into
        low-cost index funds, mutual funds, and target-date retirement funds.
      </p>
      <p>
        According to Vanguard's <em>How America Saves</em> report, consistent contributors
        who leverage compound market returns and take full advantage of their employer
        matching dollars can build substantial wealth over a 30 to 40 year career.
        This free <strong>401(k) Calculator</strong> models your wealth growth, the
        power of compound interest, employer contributions, salary raises, and sustainable
        monthly retirement income.
      </p>

      <h2>How Employer 401(k) Match Works: The 100% Instant Return</h2>
      <p>
        An employer 401(k) match is the single most valuable perk in modern
        employee benefits packages. In financial circles, financial advisors
        universally refer to it as <strong>free money</strong> that nobody should
        ever pass up. Common company match structures include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>50% Match up to 6% of Salary:</strong> If you earn $80,000 and contribute
          6% ($4,800), your company contributes an additional 3% ($2,400). That is
          an immediate 50% guaranteed return on your investment before the stock market
          even opens!
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>100% Dollar-for-Dollar Match up to 4%:</strong> If you contribute
          4% ($3,200), your company adds another $3,200, giving you an immediate 100%
          return.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            >Dollar-for-Dollar up to 3% plus 50% on next 2% (Safe Harbor Match):</strong
          > Standard formula used by many tech and corporate employers.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Golden Rule of Personal Finance:</strong> Always contribute at least
        enough to capture your full employer match before allocating money to other
        financial goals (such as an IRA, HSA, or taxable brokerage account).
      </p>

      <h2>The Power of Compound Interest in a 401(k)</h2>
      <p>
        Albert Einstein famously called compound interest the eighth wonder of
        the world. In a 401(k), the returns generated by your investments are
        reinvested back into more shares without being diminished by annual
        capital gains taxes or dividend taxes. Over several decades, the
        compounding effect accelerates dramatically:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Years 1 to 10:</strong> The vast majority of your account balance
          is composed of your own principal contributions. Progress feels slow.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Years 11 to 20:</strong> Investment earnings start matching and
          then exceeding your annual contributions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Years 21 to 35:</strong> Compounding takes over completely. Typically,
          70% to 80% or more of your ultimate retirement nest egg comes from compound
          growth rather than out-of-pocket savings.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Traditional 401(k) vs Roth 401(k): Which is Better?</h2>
      <p>
        Many employers offer both options, allowing you to split your
        contributions between Traditional and Roth accounts:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
        <table
          class="w-full text-xs text-left border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800"
        >
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 font-bold">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-2 border">Feature</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Traditional 401(k)</th>
              <th class="p-2 border">Roth 401(k)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr
              ><td class="p-2 border font-semibold">Tax Timing</td><td
                class="p-2 border">Pre-tax (tax deductible today)</td
              ><td class="p-2 border">After-tax (paid now)</td></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td class="p-2 border font-semibold">Withdrawal Taxes</td><td
                class="p-2 border">Taxed as regular income in retirement</td
              ><td class="p-2 border">100% Tax-Free (principal + gains)</td></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td class="p-2 border font-semibold">Employer Match Location</td
              ><td class="p-2 border">Traditional account (pre-tax)</td><td
                class="p-2 border"
                >Traditional account (or optional Roth under SECURE 2.0)</td
              ></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td class="p-2 border font-semibold">Early Withdrawal Rules</td
              ><td class="p-2 border">10% penalty + taxes before 59½</td><td
                class="p-2 border"
                >Contributions penalty-free; earnings penalized before 59½</td
              ></tr
            >
            <tr
              ><td class="p-2 border font-semibold"
                >Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs)</td
              ><td class="p-2 border">Yes, starting at age 73</td><td
                class="p-2 border">No RMDs (eliminated in 2024)</td
              ></tr
            >
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>2025 401(k) Contribution Limits and SECURE 2.0 Changes</h2>
      <p>
        For the tax year 2025, the IRS increased the standard employee elective
        deferral limit to <strong>$23,500</strong> (up $500 from 2024). In addition,
        employees aged 50 and older can contribute a catch-up amount of <strong
          >$7,500</strong
        >, bringing their total personal contribution limit to <strong
          >$31,000</strong
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Under the SECURE 2.0 Act, an unprecedented "super catch-up" provision
        takes effect in 2025 for employees aged 60, 61, 62, and 63. Eligible
        workers in this age bracket can contribute a catch-up of up to <strong
          >$11,250</strong
        > (or 150% of the standard catch-up), enabling a maximum personal deferral
        of <strong>$34,750</strong> in a single year.
      </p>

      <h2>How Much Money Do You Need in Your 401(k) to Retire?</h2>
      <p>
        A widely accepted benchmark is William Bengen's <strong
          >4% Safe Withdrawal Rule</strong
        >. It suggests that if you withdraw 4% of your portfolio in your first
        year of retirement and adjust for inflation each year thereafter, your
        money has a 95% probability of lasting at least 30 years:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          To generate <strong>$40,000/year</strong> ($3,333/month), you need a 401(k)
          balance of <strong>$1,000,000</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          To generate <strong>$60,000/year</strong> ($5,000/month), you need a 401(k)
          balance of <strong>$1,500,000</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          To generate <strong>$80,000/year</strong> ($6,666/month), you need a 401(k)
          balance of <strong>$2,000,000</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          To generate <strong>$100,000/year</strong> ($8,333/month), you need a 401(k)
          balance of <strong>$2,500,000</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Combine your 401(k) retirement balance with anticipated Social Security
        benefits and any pension plans to calculate your total retirement
        paycheck.
      </p>
    </article>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <details
        class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800"
        open
      >
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"
          ><h3
            class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
          >
            1. What is the maximum 401(k) contribution limit for 2025?<span
              class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3></summary
        >
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          For 2025, the employee elective deferral limit is $23,500. Workers
          aged 50 and older can contribute an additional $7,500 catch-up
          ($31,000 total). For employees aged 60 to 63, SECURE 2.0 allows a
          super catch-up of $11,250 ($34,750 total). The total combined employee
