        </p>
      </details>
      <details
        class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"
          ><h3
            class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
          >
            2. What happens to my 401(k) if I switch or leave my job?<span
              class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3></summary
        >
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          When leaving an employer, you have four main options: (1) Leave the
          funds in the existing 401(k) if your balance exceeds $7,000; (2)
          Direct rollover into your new employer's 401(k); (3) Rollover into a
          personal Traditional or Roth IRA at a brokerage like Vanguard,
          Fidelity, or Schwab; or (4) Cash out (strongly discouraged due to
          ordinary income taxes plus a 10% IRS early withdrawal penalty if under
          59½).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details
        class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"
          ><h3
            class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
          >
            3. What is vesting and how does it affect the employer match?<span
              class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3></summary
        >
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Your own contributions to your 401(k) are always 100% vested
          immediately (they belong to you forever). However, employer matching
          contributions may follow a vesting schedule (e.g., cliff vesting after
          3 years, or graded vesting where you own 20% more per year over 5
          years). If you leave before fully vesting, you forfeit the unvested
          portion of company matching funds.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details
        class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"
          ><h3
            class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
          >
            4. Can I withdraw money from my 401(k) before age 59½ without
            penalty?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
              >▼</span>
          </h3></summary
        >
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Generally, withdrawals before age 59½ incur ordinary income tax plus a
          10% early withdrawal penalty. However, IRS exceptions include: the
          Rule of 55 (if you leave your employer in or after the year you turn
          55), total permanent disability, unreimbursed medical expenses
          exceeding 7.5% of AGI, birth or adoption expenses up to $5,000, or a
          401(k) loan that you repay over 5 years.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details
        class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800"
      >
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"
          ><h3
            class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white"
          >
            5. What rate of return should I expect for my 401(k)?<span
              class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3></summary
        >
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Historically, the S&P 500 has delivered an average nominal return of
          approximately 10% annually before inflation (or ~7% after inflation).
          Financial advisors typically recommend modeling between 6% and 8% for
          a well-diversified equity portfolio, or 5% to 6% for more conservative
          bond-heavy allocations as you approach retirement.
        </p>
      </details>
